In .NET 10 (MAUI), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated/removed because Android itself deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE`. The recommended approach is now **edge-to-edge layout with `WindowInsets` handling**.

## Solution

In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Android.OS.Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        // Enable edge-to-edge
        WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, false);

        // Listen for keyboard (IME) insets and adjust padding
        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!, 
            new InsetsListener());
    }

    private class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
    {
        public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
        {
            var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
            var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

            // Use the larger of IME or nav bar bottom inset
            int bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
            v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottom);

            return insets;
        }
    }
}
```

## Why this works

- `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)` opts into edge-to-edge mode, letting your app draw behind system bars.
- The `WindowInsets` listener reacts to the IME (keyboard) appearing/disappearing and applies bottom padding to push your content up.

## Optional: animate the transition

Wrap your layout adjustment in `WindowInsetsAnimationCompat.Callback` for a smooth slide-up matching the keyboard animation, rather than a jump.

## XAML side

No special XAML changes needed — just make sure your input `Entry`/`Editor` is at the bottom of your layout (e.g., in a `Grid` or `VerticalStackLayout`). The padding adjustment on the Android content view handles the rest.
